In article <1990Jan17.024736.9275@calvin.spp.cornell.edu> johns@calvin.spp.cornell.edu.UUCP (John Sahr) writes:
>
>Our question: The SCSI standard does not require that a sequential
>access device support disconnect/reselect (and related commands such
>as save and restore data pointer).  Our largest data transfer block
...
>Because the
>disconnect/reselect adds some complexity to our design, we would like
>to avoid it, if possible.  Is this reasonable or possible for the
>ds3100?  In particular, for the purposes of the ds3100, must we
>support disconnect/reselect?
>

It has been my experience that just because some fetures of the
standard are optional, it does not preclude assumptions on the part of
systems designers that these optional features will be supported by
SCSI devices.  In evidence, I submit the case of a particular NCR
Multibus SCSI adaptor (of which I cannot remember the model number).
It seems the designers of the firmware for this adaptor expected that
devices might want to disconnect, which was fine when they were
operating as an initiator.  What they did not consider was the case
where they would operate as a target, in which case they would always
disconnect even though the initiator of the request specified that
they shouldn't.  This caused us a great deal pain until we finally
decided that to get the NCR controller to work correctly as a target,
we had to teach our initiator to always allow disconnects, even during
system initilization (which is where this problem arose).

SOAPBOX MODE ON  >>>

While this example is not of direct concern to your implementation, it
does tend point out the short-sightedness of some systems designers.
Who's to say that this product will not eventually be retargetted to
some other hardware platform, and unless your design is general enough
to deal the personality quirks of different host adaptors, it may be a
rather difficult transition.  I not saying forget the cost, but be
careful what corners you cut.

<<< SOAPBOX MODE OFF

In my opinion, however, not implementing disconnect/reselect would be
a serious flaw in any commercial product.  Ommision of a
disconnect/reselect capability can have serious performance
ramifications for other devices on the SCSI bus.  For example, it can
greatly increase both average and worst case access times for disks on
the same SCSI bus.  If this is going to be a toy destined only for a
couple of college labs, then perhaps it could be omitted.

