grr@cbmvax.commodore.com (George Robbins) writes:

>In article <137@gazette.bcm.tmc.edu> shawkins@watson.bcm.tmc.edu (Carie K Seale-Hawkins) writes:
>> 
>> Some time ago I was told by a system maintenance person that there
>> is a difference in response times on a Vax 11/750 according to whether
>> or not you are on a DZ I/O board or a DMF board.  Is this true?  I'd
>> appreciate being enlightened on the "why" of this.  

>Not a real issue...

>All common DEC serial interfaces have the same basic input structure - a
>silo (FIFO) shared between the lines.  The DZ11 doesn't have DMA support
>for output, so I presents a higher load on the system, and there is special
>code in Berkely Unix and Ultrix to simulate "DMA".  I don't think you
>are going to notice any diffference.

That's correct, if you have no other device with high interrupt load!
It's no good idea to use DZ11's if you have any DUP11 or similar devices.
DUP11's are often used for BITNET connections. You got many transmission
restarts on the DUP11 if you're DZ11 fires. You can shutdown your BITNET-link
with KERMIT! Also I think it's no good idea to use a large number of DZ11's.
You're machine would be overloaded by the output interrupts!

But if you want to install only 1-2 DZ11's and you don't have any devices
like DUP11, the DZ11's will be a cost effective solution.

Sincerely,
Klaus Steinberger



