In article <1611@atha.AthabascaU.CA> rwa@cs.AthabascaU.CA (Ross Alexander)
writes:
> I have seen code containing
>	int * ip;
>	ip = & 2;
>compile on VMS...  reminds me of B rather than C.  Rvalues don't
>have lvalues in the real world.

This was an extension to the C language that simplified system-level
programming in VMS.  Many VMS operating system routines (routines
that would be in section 2 on Unix) take arguments that are the address
a value.  In many circumstances, the argument would be a constant.
Allowing constants to be passed by address permits

	static int system_mumble = SYSTEM_MUMBLE;
	... sys$function( ... &system_mumble) ...

to be written sys$function(... &SYSTEM_MUMBLE).  I believe the compiler
doesn't permit you to write (&2 = foo), and could potentially store the
constant in a read-only area (I haven't checked that this is the case,
and haven't needed to, as it were).

The Vax C folk are very aware that this is an extension to the "standard"
K&R definition.  It predates the Ansi committee by about 5 years.  It
makes life much easier for folk programming on VMS.  You don't have to
use it.

