We're in the last stages of ordering our VAX8600 replacement.
I'm asking for two DS5400's:

     DS5400/64Mb/TK70              DS5400/64Mb/TK70
     two RA90's                    two RA90's
     extra KDA50 controller        extra KDA50 controller

We will keep four more RA disks (two RA81, two RA82) in an external
cabinet to connect to these.  All disks will be dual-ported onto both
machines, so that if one machine goes down we can push a few buttons,
reboot the other, and keep working.  (Our faculty hate having the
machines down and being unable to get at their grant proposals...)  This
configuration completely fills the QBUS slots in each 5400.

I had been thinking of a DS5810, rather than two 5400's, but discussions
with DEC led me to believe that unless I buy an HSC or the new KDM70
controller for the 5810, I won't get appreciably better I/O performance
over the pair of 5400s.  Putting two RA90's on a KDA on a QBUS is about
the same as putting two RA90's on a KDB on a VAXBI -- the limiting factor
is the controller in both cases.  A QBUS should be able to handle two
KDA controllers running flat out, at least according to the spec., and
we're a small (5 faculty and 20 students) software research shop, not a
database emporium, so I hope I/O won't be a problem for us.

The Ethernet interface on the 5810 claims better throughput than that on
the 5400; but we have two 5400's to lessen the blow.  I hope I can split
the applications between the machines to take advantage of this.  We hope
to get some of the new DEC X terminals and serve them off the 5400 pair.

So, we went for the redundancy and two-times-16-MIPS of the 5400's vs.
the expandability and one-times-18-MIPS of the 5810.  QBUS memory and
peripherals are much cheaper, too!  I can swap QBUS boards with my
existing dozen VS3200's and MVII's...
