In article <1990Jan19.100730.24926@decatl.dec.com> karsten@decatl.alf.dec.com.UUCP (Jim Karsten) writes:
> In article <137@gazette.bcm.tmc.edu> shawkins@watson.bcm.tmc.edu (Carie K Seale-Hawkins) writes:
> >
> >Some time ago I was told by a system maintenance person that there
> >is a difference in response times on a Vax 11/750 according to whether
> >or not you are on a DZ I/O board or a DMF board.  Is this true?  I'd
> >appreciate being enlightened on the "why" of this.  
> 
>   The DMF is an DMA device were the DZ is not. In ULTRIX and BSD 4.x 
>  if the number of chars through DZ in a given time reaches a given
>  number the operating system turns off the interrupt on reciver side and
>  polls the silo with each hardclock interrupt.

Remember that the DMA issue is output only.  I believe that the input
handling is basically the same for all of the UNIBUS boards, though
I haven't seen the ultrix driver source.

In a heavily loaded system the silo-polling method gives greater thruput,
since the silo-draining code involves less overhead than handling all the
"per character" interrupts.

>                                                The most impact on
>  responce time is the number of dz on a system vs dmf or other dma
>  comm devices. Also comm devices are normally at a interrupt level
>  of 4 this is the lowest unibus interrupt level and the dz also does
>  passive release to any npr (dma) device on the unibus. Considering
>  the above it is more than possible that there would be a differance
>  in responce times depending on which device your terminal was hook
>  to. The dmf being the faster.

Maybe.  I think single character echo (if that's the measure in question)
works out the same either way, since the transmit activity is started as
part of the receive interrupt processing (I could be confused).  Any
broader measures of response time are dependent on the overall interrupt
and system loading and there you might see some difference, though it
could easily be swamped by any number of other performance issues.

I don't think Carrie mentioned Ultrix or VMS - the constraints may be different
there since the BSD/Ultrix DMZ handling has been worked over a few times...

