In article <13031@watcgl.waterloo.edu> idallen@watcgl.waterloo.edu (Ian! D. Allen [CGL]) writes:
> We're in the last stages of ordering our VAX8600 replacement.
> I'm asking for two DS5400's:
...
> I had been thinking of a DS5810, rather than two 5400's, but discussions
> with DEC led me to believe that unless I buy an HSC or the new KDM70
> controller for the 5810, I won't get appreciably better I/O performance
> over the pair of 5400s.  Putting two RA90's on a KDA on a QBUS is about
> the same as putting two RA90's on a KDB on a VAXBI -- the limiting factor
> is the controller in both cases.

Well, I can't argue with the conclusion, since if somebody is willing to
spring for two machines each of which is almost as fast, the redundancy
benefits are pretty attractive.

I'm afraid that the I/O performance numbers have too much jelly to be
meaningful unless your local dec people were able to bring you into contact
with someone who has actually done some performance evaluation.  The spec
sustained thruput figures for the UDA50/KDA50/KDB50 are all in the same
ballpark, but depending on who you listen to, the UDA50 is a real dog,
the KDB50 isn't half bad and nobody talks about the KDA50.

The other shoe that hasn't really fallen yet is the DSSI drive attachment.
If DEC releases ~600MB and ~1GB DSSI drives then you could attach a lot
more disk with fewer controller slots.  DEC claims better thruput for the
DSSI bus and Q-bus DSSI controller but it's not at all clear if the 5400's
integrated DSSI controller is a high or low performance implementation.
The 5400's ethernet performance sounds pretty sleepy, but it might not be
a big deal and you might be able to improve it by sticking in a Q-bus card.

The XMI attached disk, ethernet and CI controllers sound good in the long
run for the 5800 family, but they're still imaginary when it comes to
configuring a 5800 and I've already paid for the BI versions...

> So, we went for the redundancy and two-times-16-MIPS of the 5400's vs.
> the expandability and one-times-18-MIPS of the 5810.  QBUS memory and
> peripherals are much cheaper, too!  I can swap QBUS boards with my
> existing dozen VS3200's and MVII's...

Well, I went the 5810 way.  I would have had trouble justifying two
systems (isn't one enough?) and wasn't really all that eager to have to
administer two systems to serve one function (central timesharing/mail/
news/server).  I also had access to some HSC50's and wanted (hopefully)
disk/tape performace that approximated that of my 785, which had a couple
of Sabre 1.2G drives on an Emulex SBI controller.  The upgrade to a 5820
option also sounded attractive.

Did I get the right deal?  Only time will really tell, and if it fills
the requirements and is affordable, it doesn't really too much matter.
I may get energetic one day and dual-port one of the drives between the
HSC and KDB and see which is really "faster".

The bigest problem currently is just that the silly thing don't do VAX
code and it's a lot of work to move 5-years of randomly accreted code
over.
