Well sounds like fun so I'll throw in a table of pseudo-benchmarks.

Raw Disk Read tests - reading from a single drive/controller on an
unloaded system using dd if=raw-disk of=/dev/null bs=(tracksize blocks)
raw disk was "b" partition which varied in size between drives (15-65M-byte)
and may not have been cylinder aligned on some of the RA type drives.

System			Controller	Drive		Byte/sec

750/4.3Tahoe		SC750		CDC9766		570K

785/Ultrix 3.1		MBA		RM80		726K
785/Ultrix 3.1		SBI/SC7003	Sabre 1230	1045K	8-)
785/Ultrix 3.1		UBA/UDA50	RA81		460K	8-(

5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/KDB50	RA70		675K
5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/KDB50	RA81		862K
5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/KDB50	RA90		819K
5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/KDB50/Exsys	Sabre 1123*2	813K

5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/HSC50	RA81		862K
5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/HSC50	RA90		885K
5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/HSC50/Exsys	Sabre 1123*2	741K

DS3100/Ultrix 3.1C	SCSI		RZ55		789K

Whether raw reads have any relevance as compared to a well validated
I/O benchmark I leave up to you.  It is worth noting that given enough
CPU cycles, multiple controller/drive paths add thruput as long as
you're not bus limited - on the 5810 3 RA81's running simultaneously
thru the KDB50 and two HSC50 cards yielded up 2.3M byte/sec.

Another thing to note is that performance with the RA90's and the
Exsys/Sabre drives seems to actually be slower than the RA81's in some
cases.  If don't know if this is becuase of the drive being faster
than the controller or other factors.  Interleaving anybody?

One could conclude for raw disk performance the Ideal Machine might
be something like an 8650 with a bunch of SC7003's.  Could be, as long
as you don't run out of CPU cycles.  (I dunno if the 86X0 SBI runs
at the full 13.3 Mbyte/sec rate).  You can get a lot more CPU cycles
in some of the BI boxes.

Total I/O thruput in any but the low end systems is usually more
than adequate for typical Unix systems.  DEC usually seems to do
as good a job on the System busses - SBI/BI/XMI as they do a lousey
job throttling the controllers and/or drives...

Some quick numbers on cooked and NFS thruput.  Here an unloaded 785
serving the 5810 off the Sabre 1230 drives.  All the NFS stuff pretty
much default with 4 nfsd's and 4 biod's.  Test is reading a ~7M-byte
file with dd if=file of=/dev/null bs=x.

785 Serving 5810 
bs		local		NFS
		byte/sec	byte/sec

!bs		114K		143K
1b		254K		143K
32b		572K		143K
128b		680K		143K

I'm not sure why there's a difference between the !bs and bs=1b numbers -
perhaps DEC's n-buffering is only turned on when you specify a blocksize.

Trying similar tests on the suns proved fairly difficult.  The Suns I
was playing with (a 4/280 w/7053 and Sabre 1230 drives and an Sparcstation 1)
seemed to be doing some agressive buffering which made getting repeatable
numbers difficult.  Resorting to large (~50 M-byte) files cured this, but
made things kind of slow.

All numbers using normal (non-raw) I/O, dd of=/dev/null bs=128b...

Sun 4/280 local disk	1150K
Sun 4/280 serving SS/1	594K
Sun 4/280 serving 5810	425K
Sun 4/280 serving 785	160K

5810 local disk		590K	(kdb/ra81)
5810 serving DS3100	273K
5810 serving SS/1	213K	8-(
785 serving SS/1	137K	8-(((

Obvious, NFS performance on the 785 pretty poor in comparison to the Sun 4.
It's less obvious whether this has to do with the "NFS performance problems"
that DEC supposedly fixed in 3.1C (RISC) or the poor thing just runs out of
CPU cycles.  Prior experience shows the 785 to be pretty much CPU bound
during this kind of fun.

As for the 5810, showing half the server performance of a Sun 4 needs some
kind of explaining if the 5810 is supposed to be DEC's answer to comptitive
RISC based servers.  Admittedly, it doesn't do too bad the opposite way, but
that's not exactly the meal ticket. 

Disclaimer:  I could have done all kind of stupid things or been confused
or have hardware or network problems.  Maybe somebody else can do better?

