In article <9403@cbmvax.commodore.com> grr@cbmvax.commodore.com
(George Robbins) writes:
>Well sounds like fun so I'll throw in a table of pseudo-benchmarks.

>785/Ultrix 3.1		SBI/SC7003	Sabre 1230	1045K	8-)

This is about right: the maximum MBA rate is about 1.1 MB/s, which
matches pretty closely both old SMD disks and newer `fast' 5.25 inch
disks (`fast' in quotes since these are from the same class as the
old 10 MB Quantum drive klunkers; some of the high density 5.25 inch
disks do better than 1 MB/s).

>5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/KDB50	RA81		862K

Your system does better than mine here: on an 8250 with KDB50+RA81,
running 4.3BSD-tahoe with my driver, I get only 660 kB/s.  Some of this
is no doubt due to the BI bus being used for CPU and memory traffic as
well.

>5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/KDB50	RA90		819K

>5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/HSC50	RA81		862K
>5810/Ultrix 3.1C	BI/HSC50	RA90		885K

Curious that the HSC gives no real gain.  Then again, perhaps not curious;
this may be the actual data rate from the drives.

>DS3100/Ultrix 3.1C	SCSI		RZ55		789K

RZ55s with new firmware---if you can get it, which we cannot---do better.

>Whether raw reads have any relevance as compared to a well validated
>I/O benchmark I leave up to you.

They are good for `will never exceed' speeds, and good for `how well
have I done tuning the file system / device driver?' questions.

>Another thing to note is that performance with the RA90's and the
>Exsys/Sabre drives seems to actually be slower than the RA81's in some
>cases.

All else aside, the RA90 should be faster simply because the bit rate
is higher.  The Exsys could well be slower because of MSCP protocol
conversion (the overhead for decoding the !*@% MSCP packets is
horrendous: give me straight hardware registers any day).  (SCSI is
not so great either, but at least no one uses 8085s to decode SCSI
packets.)

RA81s are much slower when used in a typical manner, since they (a)
use embedded sync (have to find those sectors every time we switch
heads) and (b) apparently have poor seek settle times.  That 800 kB/s
turns into 250 kB/s through the file system.  Fujitsu Eagles on an
Emulex MASSBUS-emulator do better, going from 1 MB/s to 500 kB/s.  A
servo track helps greatly since neither controller is smart enough
to read ahead the current cylinder.  (The HSC50 could do it; someone
needs to write clever software.  It would probably take a few days,
given the existing code, to bash out a simple read-ahead-with-preemption
algorithm.  I am also assuming the HSC50 has sufficient memory.  It
takes 357 kB to store one RA81 cylinder; it would help to have 1 MB
minimum of disk cache RAM on the HSC.  I have no idea why DEC have
not already done this....)

>One could conclude for raw disk performance the Ideal Machine might
>be something like an 8650 with a bunch of SC7003's.

8650s are too expensive, not to mention already obsoleted.  Better:
someone should make a BI board that provides raw disk registers to
the CPU, a la a MASSBUS adapter.  Writing the BI equivalent of hp.c
would not be hard.  It would also help to have a BI card that does
SCSI---not TMSCP-to-SCSI translation, not MSCP-to-SCSI translation,
just raw SCSI.  Give me direct control of the command and data packets.

(ALERT TO VENDORS who make a converter board for BI or Unibus: add
a `special mode switch' to allow direct control, and we will buy your
board today.  I do not care that the board has software to convert
MSCP, as long as I can bypass it and send SCSI commands directly.)

Incidentally, some Ultrix VAX machines with SCSI interfaces (3000
series) may have SCSI performance problems due to software translation
of MSCP to SCSI and back.  Apparently---I have been told this, but not
verified it myself---in the zeal to make every disk an MSCP disk, DEC
decided they would add a software layer over the SCSI controller to
make it look like an MSCP disk.  Waste of cycles, I say....

>(I dunno if the 86X0 SBI runs at the full 13.3 Mbyte/sec rate).

No reason it should not.  Note, though, that the BI also has a 13.3
MB/s maximum transfer rate.  Indeed, comparing the SBI and the BI
spec-for-spec shows that the two are, for all practical purposes,
identical.  Only the name and hardware implementation have changed.

>... DEC usually seems to do as good a job on the System busses -
>SBI/BI/XMI as they do a lousey job throttling the controllers and/or
>drives...

Agree.  Given the (relative lack of) CPU speed, their major busses
are either just right or overkill; their problem is with the stuff
at the far end.  (I know not why they do not give in and repackage
systems from their competitors.  Many of their Unibus controllers---
e.g., for the TU80---are already made by Dilog.)
